#{[17592186234006 :question/problem "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}{x}^{2}-3x+b\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<2 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+cx+1\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>2\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nit is known that $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)=1.$ What is the value of the constants $b$ and $c?$"] [17592186234006 :question/worked-solution "In order for $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ to be equal to $1,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $1.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={x}^{2}-3x+b.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}({x}^{2}-3x+b) \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-3(2)+b \\\\  & =4-6+b \\\\  & =b-2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left-sided limit must be equal to $1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & =1 \\\\ b-2 & =1 \\\\ b & =1+2 \\\\ b & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+cx+1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(-{x}^{2}+cx+1) \\\\  & =-{2}^{2}+c(2)+1 \\\\  & =-4+2c+1 \\\\  & =2c-3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the right-sided limit must be equal to $1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & =1 \\\\ 2c-3 & =1 \\\\ c & =\\frac{1+3}{2} \\\\ c & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186234010 :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=0$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{3x}-1}{x} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[{e}^{3x}-1]^{′}}{[x]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3{e}^{3x}}{1} \\\\  & =3{e}^{3(0)} \\\\  & =3(1) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186234014 :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x)$, the left-sided limit of $f(x)$ at $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}\\frac{k}{x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{-1-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}(2x-k) \\\\  & =2(-1)-k \\\\  & =-2-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $f(-1)=-2-k.$ \n\nSo, for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}f(x) \\\\ -2-k & =-\\frac{k}{2} \\\\ -4-2k & =-k \\\\ k & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=-4$ removes the discontinuity at $x=-1.$"] [17592186234057 :question/worked-solution "Applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+3x+4}+6x}{4x-2}=\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to 1}(\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+3x+4}+6x)}{\\lim_{x\\to 1}(4x-2)}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\nApplying the root rule in the numerator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}(\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+3x+4}+6x) & =\\sqrt[3]{{1}^{2}+3(1)+4}+6(1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{1+3+4}+6 \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8}+6 \\\\  & =2+6 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the denominator, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}(4x-2) & =4(1)-2=2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+3x+4}+6x}{4x-2} & =\\frac{8}{2}=4\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186234058 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-1}-3}{x-5} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2(5)-1}-3}{5-5}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-1}-3}{x-5} & =\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{+}}\\frac{(\\sqrt{2x-1}-3)(\\sqrt{2x-1}+3)}{(x-5)(\\sqrt{2x-1}+3)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{+}}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{2x-1})}^{2}-{3}^{2}}{(x-5)(\\sqrt{2x-1}+3)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{+}}\\frac{2x-1-9}{(x-5)(\\sqrt{2x-1}+3)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{+}}\\frac{2x-10}{(x-5)(\\sqrt{2x-1}+3)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{+}}\\frac{2(x-5)}{(x-5)(\\sqrt{2x-1}+3)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{+}}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2x-1}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2(5)-1}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186234063 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=2$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{x-2}{2x-\\sqrt{8x}} & =\\frac{2-2}{2(2)-\\sqrt{8(2)}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{x-2}{2x-\\sqrt{8x}} & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{(x-2)(2x+\\sqrt{8x})}{(2x-\\sqrt{8x})(2x+\\sqrt{8x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{(x-2)(2x+\\sqrt{8x})}{(2x)^{2}-{(\\sqrt{8x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{(x-2)(2x+\\sqrt{8x})}{4{x}^{2}-8x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{(x-2)(2x+\\sqrt{8x})}{4x(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{2x+\\sqrt{8x}}{4x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2)+\\sqrt{8(2)}}{4(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+4}{8} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186234064 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=8$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{x-8}{4-\\sqrt{2x}} & =\\frac{8-8}{4-\\sqrt{2\\cdot 8}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{x-8}{4-\\sqrt{2x}} & =\\lim_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{(x-8)(4+\\sqrt{2x})}{(4-\\sqrt{2x})(4+\\sqrt{2x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{(x-8)(4+\\sqrt{2x})}{{4}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{2x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{(x-8)(4+\\sqrt{2x})}{16-2x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{(x-8)(4+\\sqrt{2x})}{-2(x-8)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{4+\\sqrt{2x}}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+\\sqrt{2(8)}}{-2} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186234065 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=25$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 25}\\frac{x-25}{\\sqrt{x}-5} & =\\frac{25-25}{\\sqrt{25}-5}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 25}\\frac{x-25}{\\sqrt{x}-5} & =\\lim_{x\\to 25}\\frac{(x-25)(\\sqrt{x}+5)}{(\\sqrt{x}-5)(\\sqrt{x}+5)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 25}\\frac{(x-25)(\\sqrt{x}+5)}{{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}-{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 25}\\frac{(x-25)(\\sqrt{x}+5)}{x-25} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 25}(\\sqrt{x}+5) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25}+5 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186234066 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the algebra of limits to pull out the constant factor of $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\right) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)=0,\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)=2\\cdot 0=0\n$$"] [17592186234068 :question/worked-solution "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /3}\\operatorname{csc}(3x) & =\\operatorname{csc}(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}(\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}(3x).$\n\n![](images/c8/c858e8756e180eeefc965a760b9d59b0e369738dfd16b32b17b448ca0daa513e.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ from the left, the values of $y=\\operatorname{csc}(3x)$ are positive and grow without bound. So,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /3)^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}(3x)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nSimilarly, as $x$ approaches $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ from the right, the values of $y=\\operatorname{csc}(3x)$ are negative and decrease without bound. So,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /3)^{+}}\\operatorname{csc}(3x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the two limits are not equal, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /3}\\operatorname{csc}(3x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [17592186234069 :question/worked-solution "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}x & =\\operatorname{csc}0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}(x).$\n\n![](images/fc/fcdebef9e1184a2850c91e9ff1c4b6f0b47ce12b8bad07772171a7304f79150a.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches $0$ from the left, the values of $y=\\operatorname{csc}x$ are negative and decrease without bound. \n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}x=-\\infty \n$$"] [17592186234070 :question/worked-solution "Let's introduce the new variable $t=8x.$ In the limit $x\\to 0,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & \\to 8(0)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\operatorname{cot}8x=\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cot}t=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}t$ is given below.\n\n![](images/02/023929a3b0d4d40c7bd86a22ea04965d63e455ea3fc8d524c84314cb8fefdaac.png)"] [17592186234076 :question/worked-solution "First, we let\n\n$$\nL=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2+h}-{e}^{2}}{h}.\n$$\n\nComparing this with the formula for the instantaneous rate of change\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h}.\n$$\n\nWe see that if we let $f(x)={e}^{x}$ and $a=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2) & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(2+h)-f(2)}{h} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2+h}-{e}^{2}}{h}=L.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis confirms that $L={f}^{′}(2).$  \n\nComputing the derivative, we have\n \n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{f}^{′}(2)={e}^{2},\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude tha $L={e}^{2}.$"] [17592186234107 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{l})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{l}=a+lΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={x}^{2},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{n}=\\frac{1}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{l})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[f(1+l\\cdot \\frac{1}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{l}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{l}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186234125 :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}8x-1}{2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(8\\cdot 0)-1}{2\\cdot 0}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\theta }=0\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}8x-1}{2x} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{-(1-\\operatorname{cos}8x)}{x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}8x}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}8x}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}(8\\cdot \\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}8x}{8\\cdot x}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}8x}{8x} \\\\  & =-4\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}8x}{8x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $\\theta =8x.$ Then, since $\\theta \\to 0$ as $x\\to 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}8x}{8x}=\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\theta }=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}8x}{8x} & =-4\\cdot 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186234238 :question/worked-solution "First, let's calculate ${x}^{2}\\cdot p(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}}\\right)=\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nWhen we divide the two functions ${x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)$ and $q(x)$, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)}{q(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{2}{3{x}^{3}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\cdot (8) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }-12 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186234242 :question/worked-solution "Here, we apply the quotient rule and the power rule to get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{z\\to -1}\\frac{(z-2)^{4}}{(z+2)^{3}} & =\\frac{\\lim_{z\\to -1}(z-2)^{4}}{\\lim_{z\\to -1}(z+2)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\lim_{z\\to -1}(z-2))}^{4}}{{(\\lim_{z\\to -1}(z+2))}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1-2)^{4}}{(-1+2)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3)^{4}}{{1}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{1} \\\\  & =81\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186234243 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that $\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi /2}f(x)=1.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi /2}(4x)^{2}(f(x)-2)^{4}=\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi /2}(4x)^{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /2}(f(x)-2)^{4}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi /2}(4x)^{2} & ={(\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi /2}(4x))}^{2} \\\\  & =(4\\cdot (\\pi /2))^{2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{2} \\\\  & =4{\\pi }^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi /2}(f(x)-2)^{4} & ={(\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi /2}(f(x)-2))}^{4} \\\\  & =(1-2)^{4} \\\\  & =(-1)^{4} \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi /2}(4x)^{2}(f(x)-2)^{4} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi /2}(4x)^{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /2}(f(x)-2)^{4} \\\\  & =(4{\\pi }^{2})\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =4{\\pi }^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186234244 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that $\\lim_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=9.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,3}(3x-10)^{3}(f(x)-x)^{2}=\\lim_{x\\to \\,3}(3x-10)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,3}(f(x)-x)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,3}(3x-10)^{3} & ={(\\lim_{x\\to \\,3}(3x-10))}^{3} \\\\  & =(3(3)-10)^{3} \\\\  & =(-1)^{3} \\\\  & =-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,3}(f(x)-x)^{2} & ={(\\lim_{x\\to \\,3}(f(x)-x))}^{2} \\\\  & =(9-3)^{2} \\\\  & ={6}^{2} \\\\  & =36\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,3}(3x-10)^{3}(f(x)-x)^{2} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,3}(3x-10)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,3}(f(x)-x)^{2} \\\\  & =(-1)(36) \\\\  & =-36\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186234245 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=4.$ \n\nApplying the root rule, we get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,-2}\\sqrt{17-2f(x)} & =\\sqrt{\\lim_{x\\to \\,-2}(17-2f(x))} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17-2\\cdot (4)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186234297 :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ given below have a discontinuity at $x=-2?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}-\\frac{2}{x+2}, & x<-2 \\\\ \\frac{2}{x+2}, & x>-2\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186234321 :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}({x}^{2})}{{x}^{3}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[1-\\operatorname{cos}({x}^{2})]^{'}}{[{x}^{3}]^{'}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x\\operatorname{sin}({x}^{2})}{3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}({x}^{2})}{x\\cdot x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\cancel{x}\\operatorname{sin}({x}^{2})}{\\cancel{x}\\cdot x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}({x}^{2})}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}({x}^{2})}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}({x}^{2})}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[\\operatorname{sin}({x}^{2})]^{'}}{[x]^{'}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x\\operatorname{cos}({x}^{2})}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{2(0)\\operatorname{cos}({0}^{2})}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186234322 :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{csc}x=\\infty $ and $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{e}^{\\operatorname{csc}x}=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{csc}x}} & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{csc}x]}^{'}}{[{e}^{\\operatorname{csc}x}]^{'}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{-\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{csc}x}(-\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\cancel{-\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x}}{{e}^{\\operatorname{csc}x}\\cancel{(-\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x)}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{csc}x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186234323 :question/worked-solution "Notice that if we try to substitute $x=1$ directly into the limit, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{f(x)+x}{g(x)-x} & ={\\frac{f(x)+x}{g(x)-x}|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(1)+1}{g(1)-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we use L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{f(x)+x}{g(x)-x} & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(f(x)+x)^{′}}{(g(x)-x)^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)+1}{{g}^{′}(x)-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=1$ again, but once more, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)+1}{{g}^{′}(x)-1} & ={\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)+1}{{g}^{′}(x)-1}|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)+1}{{g}^{′}(1)-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply L'Hopital's rule again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)+1}{{g}^{′}(x)-1} & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{({f}^{′}(x)+1)^{′}}{({g}^{′}(x)-1)^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this time, when we substitute $x=1,$ we are actually able to determine the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)} & ={\\frac{{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)}|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{{g}^{″}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{-2} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{f(x)+x}{g(x)-x}=-2.$"] [17592186234333 :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=-2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=f(-2)=-3.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}(3f(x)-xf(-2)) & =3\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)-f(-2)\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}x \\\\  & =3(-3)-(-3)(-2) \\\\  & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186234340 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{9}\\operatorname{ln}{x}^{2/3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}{x}^{2/3},$ the lower bound is $a=4,$ and the upper bound is $b=9.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{9-4}{n}=\\frac{5}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{9}\\operatorname{ln}{x}^{2/3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(4+i\\cdot \\frac{5}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(4+\\frac{5i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{ln}({(4+\\frac{5i}{n})}^{2/3})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186234344 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}f({x}_{m})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{m}=a+mΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{7}(3x-7)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=(3x-7)^{3},$ the lower bound is $a=4$ and the upper bound is $b=7.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{7-4}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{7}(3x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}f({x}_{m})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[f(4+m\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[f(4+\\frac{3m}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[{(3(4+\\frac{3m}{n})-7)}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[{(5+\\frac{9m}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186234352 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[-2,-1].$ In this interval, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,-1].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider the interval $[-1,1].$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we note that the interval $(0,1]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only\"."] [17592186234369 :question/worked-solution "Let's compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1} & =-\\sqrt{5} \\\\ {s}_{2} & =-\\sqrt{5}+(-\\sqrt{5})=-2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ {s}_{3} & =-\\sqrt{5}+(-\\sqrt{5})+(-\\sqrt{5})=-3\\sqrt{5} \\\\ {s}_{4} & =-\\sqrt{5}+(-\\sqrt{5})+(-\\sqrt{5})+(-\\sqrt{5})=-4\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the sequence of partial sums, we can see that the formula for the $n$th partial sum is \n\n$$\n{s}_{n}=-\\sqrt{5}\\,n.\n$$\n\nThe sequence of partial sums is divergent:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{s}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }(-\\sqrt{5}\\,n)=-\\infty \n$$\n\nTherefore, the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\sqrt{5})\n$$ is also divergent."] [17592186234370 :question/worked-solution "Let's compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1} & =0 \\\\ {s}_{2} & =0+1=1 \\\\ {s}_{3} & =1-2=-1 \\\\ {s}_{4} & =-1-2=-3 \\\\ {s}_{5} & =-3-2=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the sequence of partial sums, we can see that the formula for the $n$th partial sum for $n\\ge 3$ is\n\n$$\n{s}_{n}=1-2(n-2)=5-2n.\n$$\n\nThe sequence of partial sums is divergent:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{s}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }(5-2n)=-\\infty \n$$\n\nTherefore, the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ diverges."] [17592186234393 :question/worked-solution "Here, we apply the quotient rule and the power rule to get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(2x+1)^{3}}{(x-2)^{5}} & =\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to 1}(2x+1)^{3}}{\\lim_{x\\to 1}(x-2)^{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\lim_{x\\to 1}(2x+1))}^{3}}{{(\\lim_{x\\to 1}(x-2))}^{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2(1)+1)^{3}}{(1-2)^{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3)^{3}}{(-1)^{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{-1} \\\\  & =-27\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186234394 :question/worked-solution "Applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 4}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}-1}{3x-2}=\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to 4}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}-1)}{\\lim_{x\\to 4}(3x-2)}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\nApplying the root rule in the numerator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 4}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}-1) & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+9}-1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+9}-1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25}-1 \\\\  & =5-1 \\\\  & =4\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the denominator, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 4}(3x-2) & =3(4)-2 \\\\  & =10\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 4}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}-1}{3x-2} & =\\frac{4}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186234395 :question/worked-solution "Using the power and quotient rules, we can rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(2f(x)+3)^{8}}{(f(x)+4)^{3}} & =\\frac{{(\\lim_{x\\to 0}(2f(x)+3))}^{8}}{{(\\lim_{x\\to 0}(f(x)+4))}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above is true provided that  $\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ exists.\n\nWe find from the graph that $\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=-2.$ We calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}(2f(x)+3)=2(-2)+3=-1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}(f(x)+4)=-2+4=2\n$$\n\nSo we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(2f(x)+3)^{8}}{(f(x)+4)^{3}} & =\\frac{{(\\lim_{x\\to 0}(2f(x)+3))}^{8}}{{(\\lim_{x\\to 0}(f(x)+4))}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)^{8}}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186234396 :question/worked-solution "Using the power and quotient rules, we can rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(3f(x)-1)^{2}}{(f(x)-1)^{3}} & =\\frac{{(\\lim_{x\\to 2}(3f(x)-1))}^{2}}{{(\\lim_{x\\to 2}(f(x)-1))}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above is true provided that  $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ exists.\n\nWe find from the graph that $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)=-1.$ We calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2}(3f(x)-1)=3(-1)-1=-4\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2}(f(x)-1)=-1-1=-2\n$$\n\nSo we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(3f(x)-1)^{2}}{(f(x)-1)^{3}} & =\\frac{{(\\lim_{x\\to 2}(3f(x)-1))}^{2}}{{(\\lim_{x\\to 2}(f(x)-1))}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-4)^{2}}{(-2)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{-8} \\\\  & =-2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186234397 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that  $\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=1.$ \n\nApplying the root rule, we get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}\\frac{\\sqrt{8f(x)+1}}{2(f(x))^{5}} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}(8f(x)+1)}}{2{(\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x))}^{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{8(1)+1}}{2\\cdot (1)^{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{9}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186234508 :question/worked-solution "The graph $y={x}^{6}$ looks similar to an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{6}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, multiplying by the negative coefficient $-\\frac{1}{6}$ causes $y=-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}$ to look more like a downward parabola. This changes the sign of the limit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}\\right)=-\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{6})=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186234509 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits,  as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{9}}\\right) & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{9}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the fact that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{9}}\\right)=-\\infty ,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{9}}\\right)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{9}}\\right)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n-6\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{9}}\\right)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [17592186234511 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{3}{7(x-1)^{4}} & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(\\frac{3}{7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x-1)^{4}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{4}}$ as follows:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}.$\n\n- Translate it to the right by $1$ unit. This gives $y=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{4}},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc826419a3212b7ce5028a59c90ae43c826734719249119ac3be08386bae4481.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{4}}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nThe positive factor of $\\frac{3}{7}$ does not change the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{4}}=\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186234520 :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=-1$ into the expression, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\sqrt[3]{4x+5} & =\\sqrt[3]{4(-1)+5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186234522 :question/worked-solution "According to the sum and constant multiple rules, we can compute the required limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}({x}^{3}-5f(x))=\\lim_{x\\to 0}{x}^{3}-5\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)\n$$\n\n \n\nThe above assumes that $\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ exists.\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=2.$ \nSo we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}({x}^{3}-5f(x)) & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}{x}^{3}-5\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x) \\\\  & ={0}^{3}-5(2) \\\\  & =0-10 \\\\  & =-10\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186234523 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=0$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\operatorname{csc}(x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{csc}(0+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186234524 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}1 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & \\text{ for }\\,x>1.\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186234524 :question/worked-solution "The right-sided limit $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist since $f(x)$ is not defined on the interval $x\\in (0,1).$ \n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nApproaching $x=1$ from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\sqrt{x} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235350 :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=1$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{y(x)-2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)} & =\\frac{2-2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{y(x)-2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{[y(x)-2]^{′}}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{y}^{′}(x)}{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(1)}{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (y(2))^{2}}{-\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1\\cdot {2}^{2}}{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235372 :question/worked-solution "We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}-2{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate the indefinite integral first. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}-2\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{x-3}{2x+1}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the improper integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{x-3}{2x+1}|]}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{a-3}{2a+1}|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1}{9}|) \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1-\\frac{3}{a}}{2+\\frac{1}{a}}|)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{9}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡9 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235378 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see $\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=4.$\n\nWe factor the constant out of the limit and evaluate the remaining limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}Cf(x) & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ C\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x) & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ C\\cdot 4 & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235379 :question/worked-solution "According to the sum and constant multiple rules, we can compute the required limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}(7f(x)+8g(x)-2{x}^{3})=7\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)+8\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)-2\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}{x}^{3}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe above equation assumes that $\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)$ both exist.\n\nIn part 5, we found that $\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=4,$ and from part 3, we found $\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)=-1.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}(7f(x)+8g(x)-2{x}^{3}) & =7\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)+8\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)-2\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}{x}^{3} \\\\  & =7\\cdot 4+8\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =28-8-16 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235412 :question/worked-solution "We identify the leading term of the polynomial and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }(4-{x}^{2}) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }(-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-(\\infty ) \\\\  & =-\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235413 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit directly by substituting $x=2,$ we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{x}}{f(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{x}}{4-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{2}}{4-{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since there is a radical in the numerator, we can evaluate the limit by first multiplying the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{x}}{f(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{x}}{4-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}\\frac{(\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{x})(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x})}{(4-{x}^{2})(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}\\frac{2-x}{(2+x)(2-x)(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}\\frac{\\cancel{2-x}}{(2+x)\\cancel{(2-x)}(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}\\frac{1}{(2+x)(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(2+2)(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4(2\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235414 :question/worked-solution "Simplifying the expression under the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{f(x)-f(2)}{x-2} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{[4-{x}^{2}]-[4-{2}^{2}]}{x-2} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{4-{x}^{2}}{x-2} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{-({x}^{2}-4)}{x-2} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{-(x+2)(x-2)}{x-2} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{-(x+2)\\cancel{(x-2)}}{\\cancel{x-2}} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235415 :question/worked-solution "We first apply the sum rule, and then apply the constant rule and compute the limits of the power functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}\\,(x+2) & =-\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}x-\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}2 \\\\  & =-2-2 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235416 :question/worked-solution "Simplifying the expression under the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{h\\to \\,0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to \\,0}\\frac{4-(x+h)^{2}-[4-{x}^{2}]}{h} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to \\,0}\\frac{4-{x}^{2}-2xh-{h}^{2}-4+{x}^{2}}{h} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to \\,0}\\frac{-2xh-{h}^{2}}{h} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to \\,0}\\frac{h(-2x-h)}{h} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to \\,0}\\frac{\\cancel{h}(-2x-h)}{\\cancel{h}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to \\,0}(-2x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235417 :question/worked-solution "We first apply the sum rule, and then apply the constant rule and compute the limits of the power functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to \\,0}(-2x-h) & =\\lim_{h\\to \\,0}(-2x)-\\lim_{h\\to \\,0}h \\\\  & =-2x\\lim_{h\\to \\,0}1-\\lim_{h\\to \\,0}h \\\\  & =-2x-0 \\\\  & =-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235421 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}5sin⁡(2x) \\\\  & =5sin⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot sin⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235425 :question/worked-solution "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{1}{5sin⁡(2x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5sin⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5sin⁡(\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{1}{5sin⁡(2x)}=\\frac{1}{5}csc⁡(2x).\n$$\n\n![](images/e3/e3f5c484211ef0b9cf5f3a29198d25c23933a1c07427d0dcbfad01edbb300d0e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, as $x$ approaches $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ from the left, the values of $y=\\frac{1}{5}csc⁡(2x)$ are positive and increase without bound. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}=\\lim_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{1}{5}csc⁡(2x)=\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186235426 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}=\\frac{1-1}{(1)^{2}-3(1)+2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2} & =\\frac{x-1}{(x-1)(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x-1}}{\\cancel{(x-1)}(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{1}{x-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-2} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235427 :question/worked-solution "Previously, we found that \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the product rule to the original limit, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{(x-1)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-3x+2} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}[\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}\\cdot f(x)] \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}5sin⁡(2x) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 5sin⁡(2\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =-5\\cdot sin⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235428 :question/worked-solution "In part 1, we found that \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x)=5.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}\\frac{2f(x)}{(1+4x)^{3}} & =\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}(2f(x))}{\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}(1+4x)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, \n\n* applying the constant multiple rule in the numerator, and \n\n* the root rule, followed by the sum rule in the denominator, \n\nwe obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}(2f(x))}{\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}(1+4x)^{3}} & =\\frac{2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x)}{{(\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}(1+4x))}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x)}{{(1+\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}(4x))}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 5}{{(1+4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{(1+\\pi )^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235446 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $t=2\\pi $ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}v(t) & =\\lim_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}2{csc}^{2}⁡(t) \\\\  & =2{csc}^{2}⁡(2\\pi ) \\\\  & =2\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t.$\n\n![](images/36/361e8bf3b622fc2e73be712dae4ec5d189bd15eecbd33c9295db7365bcf71943.png)\n\nAs $t$ approaches $2\\pi $ from the left, the values of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t$ are negative and decrease without bound. \n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=-\\infty \n$$\n\nApplying the product and power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}2{csc}^{2}⁡(2\\pi ) & =\\lim_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}2\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}{csc}^{2}⁡(t) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {(\\lim_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t))}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying by the positive constant $2$ does not change the sign of the limit, so we have\n\n$$\n2\\cdot {(\\lim_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t))}^{2}=\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186235450 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 5\\pi /2}{v}_{2}(t) & =\\lim_{t\\to 5\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235467 :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)$ exists.\n\nIn order for $\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)$ to exist, the left- and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal.\n\n* Approaching from the left, we have $x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+x.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}({e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+x) \\\\  & ={e}^{1}+{1}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =e+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n* Approaching from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=x+a.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(x+a) \\\\  & =1+a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the left and right-sided limits must be equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) \\\\ 1+a & =e+2 \\\\ a & =e+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235468 :question/worked-solution "In part 1, we found that $\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=e+2\\ne 0.$\n\nApplying the quotient rule for limits, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2}{f(x)} & =\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to 1}{x}^{3}+2}{\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{1}^{3}+2}{e+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{e+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235476 :question/worked-solution "We want to express the area $A$ between $f(x)={x}^{2}-2x$ and the $x$-axis between $x=4$ and $x=7$ as the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{4}^{7}({x}^{2}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral above, the function is $f(x)={x}^{2}-2x,$ the lower bound is $a=4,$ and the upper bound is $b=7.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{7-4}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{7}({x}^{2}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[f(4+k\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[f(4+\\frac{3k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{(4+\\frac{3k}{n})}^{2}-2(4+\\frac{3k}{n})]\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235507 :question/worked-solution "Using the quotient rule, we have \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{f(x)-{x}^{2}}{{f}^{′}(x)+2{e}^{x}}=\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,(f(x)-{x}^{2})}{\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,({f}^{′}(x)+2{e}^{x})}.\n$$\n\nWe find from the graph that $\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,f(x)=f(0)=0$ and $\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,{f}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(0)=-1.$ Calculating the limits of the numerator and denominator separately, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,(f(x)-{x}^{2})=0+{0}^{2}=0, \\\\  & \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,({f}^{′}(x)+2{e}^{x})=-1+2=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking the quotient of these limits, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{f(x)-{x}^{2}}{{f}^{′}(x)+2{e}^{x}} & =\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,(f(x)-{x}^{2})}{\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,({f}^{′}(x)+2{e}^{x})} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235508 :question/worked-solution "Notice that, to calculate $g(-7),$ the lower and upper limits are the same. So, the integral evaluates to zero:\n\n$$\ng(-7)={\\int }_{-7}^{-7}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=0\n$$\n\nSo, plugging in $x=-7$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -7}\\,\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}+6x-7} & =\\lim_{x\\to -7}\\,\\frac{{g}^{′}(x)}{[{x}^{2}+6x-7]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -7}\\,\\frac{{g}^{′}(x)}{2x+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{g}^{′}(-7)}{2\\cdot (-7)+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(-7)}{-14+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235574 :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1]?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x+\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}},\\,g(x)=\\begin{cases}-4x+1, & x<0 \\\\ -4x+3, & x\\ge 0\\end{cases}\\,,\\,h(x)=x\\sqrt[5]{x}\n$$"] [17592186235574 :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is **piecewise smooth** on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $[a,b],$ and\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists and is continuous except possibly at finitely many points, where its one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)=2x+\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0$ into the two open intervals $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1).$ For $x\\ne 0,$\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x+{x}^{2/5},\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2+\\frac{2}{5{x}^{3/5}}.\n$$\n\nWhile this derivative is continuous on the open pieces $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ the limits of the derivative as $x\\to 0$ are not finite. In fact, the derivative becomes unbounded near $x=0.$ Thus, the condition for finite limits at the endpoints is not met.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0.$ On each open interval, $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ the function is linear. On these intervals,\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\begin{cases}-4x+1, & x<0 \\\\ -4x+3, & x>0\\end{cases}\\,,\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)=\\begin{cases}-4, & x<0 \\\\ -4, & x>0\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nHere, $g(x)$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ are piecewise continuous on each open interval, and they both have finite limits at the endpoints of the subintervals.\n\n- The function $h(x)=x\\sqrt[5]{x}$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ Since\n\n$$\nh(x)={x}^{6/5},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{6}{5}{x}^{1/5}.\n$$\n\nHere, $h(x)$ and ${h}^{′}(x)$ are continuous on $[-1,1].$ Thus, $h(x)$ is continuously differentiable on the whole interval, and hence piecewise smooth.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ and $h$ only.\""] [17592186235575 :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise smooth on $x\\in [0,1]?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}3x-1, & x<\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 3x+2, & x\\ge \\frac{1}{2}\\end{cases}\\,,\\,g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{x}),\\,h(x)=\\sqrt[4]{x}\n$$"] [17592186235575 :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is **piecewise smooth** on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $[a,b],$ and\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists and is continuous except possibly at finitely many points, where its one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in [0,1].$ We split the interval at $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ On each open interval, $(0,\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},1),$ the function is linear. On these intervals,\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}3x-1, & x<\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 3x+2, & x>\\frac{1}{2}\\end{cases}\\,,\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{cases}3, & x<\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 3, & x>\\frac{1}{2}\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ are piecewise continuous on each open interval, and they both have finite limits at the endpoints of the subintervals.\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{x})$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in [0,1].$ For $x\\in (0,1),$\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{x})}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\n$$\n\nWhile this derivative is continuous on the open piece $(0,1),$ the limit of the derivative as $x\\to {0}^{+}$ is not finite. In fact, the derivative becomes unbounded near $x=0.$ Thus, the condition for finite limits at the endpoints is not met.\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\sqrt[4]{x}$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in [0,1].$ For $x\\in (0,1),$\n\n$$\nh(x)={x}^{1/4},\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{4{x}^{3/4}}.\n$$\n\nWhile this derivative is continuous on the open piece $(0,1),$ the limit of the derivative as $x\\to {0}^{+}$ is not finite. In fact, the derivative becomes unbounded near $x=0.$ Thus, the condition for finite limits at the endpoints is not met.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ only.\""] [17592186235576 :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1]?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}4x-3, & x<0 \\\\ -2x+5, & x\\ge 0\\end{cases}\\,,\\,g(x)={x}^{3}-\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}},\\,h(x)={x}^{3}\\sqrt[3]{x}\n$$"] [17592186235576 :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is **piecewise smooth** on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $[a,b],$ and\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists and is continuous except possibly at finitely many points, where its one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0.$ On each open interval, $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ the function is linear. On these intervals,\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}4x-3, & x<0 \\\\ -2x+5, & x>0\\end{cases}\\,,\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{cases}4, & x<0 \\\\ -2, & x>0\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ are piecewise continuous on each open interval, and they both have finite limits at the endpoints of the subintervals.\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{3}-\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0$ into the two open intervals $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1).$ For $x\\ne 0,$\n\n$$\ng(x)={x}^{3}-{x}^{3/5},\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{5{x}^{2/5}}.\n$$\n\nWhile this derivative is continuous on the open pieces $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ the limits of the derivative as $x\\to 0$ are not finite. In fact, the derivative becomes unbounded near $x=0.$ Thus, the condition for finite limits at the endpoints is not met.\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{3}\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ Since\n\n$$\nh(x)={x}^{10/3},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{10}{3}{x}^{7/3}.\n$$\n\nHere, $h(x)$ and ${h}^{′}(x)$ are continuous on $[-1,1].$ Thus, $h(x)$ is continuously differentiable on the whole interval, and hence piecewise smooth.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ and $h$ only.\""] [17592186235577 :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1]?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}4x-1, & x<0 \\\\ 4x+3, & x\\ge 0\\end{cases}\\,,\\,g(x)={x}^{2}+\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}},\\,h(x)={x}^{2}\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{4}}\n$$"] [17592186235577 :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is **piecewise smooth** on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $[a,b],$ and\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists and is continuous except possibly at finitely many points, where its one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0.$ On each open interval, $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ the function is linear. On these intervals,\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}4x-1, & x<0 \\\\ 4x+3, & x>0\\end{cases}\\,,\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{cases}4, & x<0 \\\\ 4, & x>0\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ are piecewise continuous on each open interval, and they both have finite limits at the endpoints of the subintervals.\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{2}+\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ Since\n\n$$\ng(x)={x}^{2}+\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}={x}^{2}+{x}^{4/3},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)=2x+\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{1/3}.\n$$\n\nHere, $g(x)$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ are continuous on $[-1,1].$ Thus, $g(x)$ is continuously differentiable on the whole interval, and hence piecewise smooth.\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{2}\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{4}}$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ Since\n\n$$\nh(x)={x}^{2}\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{4}}={x}^{14/5},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{14}{5}{x}^{9/5}.\n$$\n\nHere, $h(x)$ and ${h}^{′}(x)$ are continuous on $[-1,1].$ Thus, $h(x)$ is continuously differentiable on the whole interval, and hence piecewise smooth.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f,$ $g,$ and $h.$\""] [17592186235578 :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1]?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}\\sqrt[7]{{x}^{2}},\\,g(x)={x}^{3}+\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{8}},\\,h(x)=\\begin{cases}5x-3, & x<0 \\\\ 5x+4, & x\\ge 0\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186235578 :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is **piecewise smooth** on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $[a,b],$ and\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists and is continuous except possibly at finitely many points, where its one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{2}\\sqrt[7]{{x}^{2}}$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ Since\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}\\sqrt[7]{{x}^{2}}={x}^{16/7},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{16}{7}{x}^{9/7}.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ are continuous on $[-1,1].$ Thus, $f(x)$ is continuously differentiable on the whole interval, and hence piecewise smooth.\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{3}+\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{8}}$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ Since\n\n$$\ng(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{8/5},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}+\\frac{8}{5}{x}^{3/5}.\n$$\n\nHere, $g(x)$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ are continuous on $[-1,1].$ Thus, $g(x)$ is continuously differentiable on the whole interval, and hence piecewise smooth.\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0.$ On each open interval, $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ the function is linear. On these intervals,\n\n$$\nh(x)=\\begin{cases}5x-3, & x<0 \\\\ 5x+4, & x>0\\end{cases}\\,,\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\begin{cases}5, & x<0 \\\\ 5, & x>0\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nHere, $h(x)$ and ${h}^{′}(x)$ are piecewise continuous on each open interval, and they both have finite limits at the endpoints of the subintervals.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f,$ $g,$ and $h.$\""] [17592186235579 :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1]?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}},\\,g(x)=\\begin{cases}{x}^{2}+1, & x<0 \\\\ \\sqrt[7]{{x}^{3}}, & x\\ge 0\\end{cases}\\,,\\,h(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}})\n$$"] [17592186235579 :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is **piecewise smooth** on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $[a,b],$ and\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists and is continuous except possibly at finitely many points, where its one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0$ into the two open intervals $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1).$ For $x\\ne 0,$\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{3/5},\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3}{5{x}^{2/5}}.\n$$\n\nWhile this derivative is continuous on the open pieces $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ the limits of the derivative as $x\\to 0$ are not finite. In fact, the derivative becomes unbounded near $x=0.$ Thus, the condition for finite limits at the endpoints is not met.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0.$ On each open interval, $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\begin{cases}{x}^{2}+1, & x<0 \\\\ {x}^{3/7}, & x>0\\end{cases}\\,,\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)=\\begin{cases}2x, & x<0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{7{x}^{4/7}}, & x>0\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nHere, $g(x)$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ are continuous on each open interval, but the limit of ${g}^{′}(x)$ as $x\\to {0}^{+}$ is not finite because $\\frac{3}{7{x}^{4/7}}$ becomes unbounded. Thus, the condition for finite limits at the endpoints is not met.\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}})$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0$ into the two open intervals $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1).$ For $x\\ne 0,$\n\n$$\nh(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2/3}),\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2/3})\\cdot \\frac{2}{3{x}^{1/3}}.\n$$\n\nWhile this derivative is continuous on the open pieces $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ the limits of the derivative as $x\\to 0$ are not finite. Because $\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2/3})$ approaches $1$ while $\\frac{2}{3{x}^{1/3}}$ becomes unbounded near $x=0,$ the derivative gets infinitely large. Thus, the condition for finite limits at the endpoints is not met.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the functions.\""] [17592186235580 :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1]?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}2x-\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}, & x<0 \\\\ x+\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}, & x\\ge 0\\end{cases}\\,,\\,g(x)=4x-\\sqrt[7]{{x}^{3}},\\,h(x)=3{x}^{2}+\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}\n$$"] [17592186235580 :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is **piecewise smooth** on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $[a,b],$ and\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists and is continuous except possibly at finitely many points, where its one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0.$ On the open intervals $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}2x-{x}^{2/3}, & x<0 \\\\ x+{x}^{2/5}, & x>0\\end{cases}\\,,\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{cases}2-\\frac{2}{3{x}^{1/3}}, & x<0 \\\\ 1+\\frac{2}{5{x}^{3/5}}, & x>0\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nWhile this derivative is continuous on the open pieces $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ the limits of the derivative as $x\\to 0$ are not finite. In fact, the derivative becomes unbounded near $x=0.$ Thus, the condition for finite limits at the endpoints is not met.\n\n- The function $g(x)=4x-\\sqrt[7]{{x}^{3}}$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0$ into the two open intervals $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1).$ For $x\\ne 0,$\n\n$$\ng(x)=4x-{x}^{3/7},\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)=4-\\frac{3}{7{x}^{4/7}}.\n$$\n\nWhile this derivative is continuous on the open pieces $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ the limits of the derivative as $x\\to 0$ are not finite. In fact, the derivative becomes unbounded near $x=0.$ Thus, the condition for finite limits at the endpoints is not met.\n\n- The function $h(x)=3{x}^{2}+\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0$ into the two open intervals $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1).$ For $x\\ne 0,$\n\n$$\nh(x)=3{x}^{2}+{x}^{3/5},\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=6x+\\frac{3}{5{x}^{2/5}}.\n$$\n\nWhile this derivative is continuous on the open pieces $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ the limits of the derivative as $x\\to 0$ are not finite. In fact, the derivative becomes unbounded near $x=0.$ Thus, the condition for finite limits at the endpoints is not met.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the functions.\""] [17592186236505 :answer/value "q(x)=\\begin{cases}x-1 & x<1 \\\\ x+1, & x\\ge 1\\end{cases}"] [17592186236582 :answer/value "q(x)=\\begin{cases}2x,\\, & x<-1 \\\\ x-1,\\, & x\\ge -1\\end{cases}"] [17592186237784 :question/worked-solution "Using the power and quotient rules, we can rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -2}\\frac{(4-3f(x))^{5}}{(6-f(x))^{3}} & =\\frac{{(\\lim_{x\\to -2}(4-3f(x)))}^{5}}{{(\\lim_{x\\to -2}(6-f(x)))}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above is true provided that  $\\lim_{x\\to -2}f(x)$ exists.\n\nWe find from the graph that $\\lim_{x\\to -2}f(x)=2.$ We calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -2}(4-3f(x))=4-3(2)=-2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -2}(6-f(x))=6-2=4\n$$\n\nSo we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -2}\\frac{(4-3f(x))^{5}}{(6-f(x))^{3}} & =\\frac{{(\\lim_{x\\to -2}(4-3f(x)))}^{5}}{{(\\lim_{x\\to -2}(6-f(x)))}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)^{5}}{{4}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-32}{64} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186237786 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {e}^{px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p}{e}^{px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}2{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-3x}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{3a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{e}^{3x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{3x}}=\\infty $\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{3x}}=0$\n\n![](images/45/45e8ddd8ddb33dc8d6a701ed75878271b331ce6b3591ba8fae9f4fa49020c12b.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{3a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186237788 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{2}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-{e}^{-u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](images/48/4844d6220d04a6a1fffdd5a485a9e6724aa6065397b8c3a009157a7a2dafc3cf.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186237800 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{2z+1}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{2z+1}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{3}{2}(2z+1)^{2/3}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[(2z+1)^{2/3}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }((2a+1)^{2/3}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent."] [17592186237801 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{2-3t}} & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{2-3t}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{2-3t}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\sqrt{2+3}-\\sqrt{2-3a}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }\\sqrt{2-3a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}+\\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent."] [17592186237815 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=2$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x-2}{\\sqrt{3x+1}-\\sqrt{7}} & =\\frac{2-2}{\\sqrt{3(2)+1}-\\sqrt{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-2}{\\sqrt{7}-\\sqrt{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x-2}{\\sqrt{3x+1}-\\sqrt{7}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(x-2)(\\sqrt{3x+1}+\\sqrt{7})}{(\\sqrt{3x+1}-\\sqrt{7})(\\sqrt{3x+1}+\\sqrt{7})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(x-2)(\\sqrt{3x+1}+\\sqrt{7})}{(\\sqrt{3x+1})^{2}-(\\sqrt{7})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(x-2)(\\sqrt{3x+1}+\\sqrt{7})}{3x+1-7} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(x-2)(\\sqrt{3x+1}+\\sqrt{7})}{3x-6} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(x-2)(\\sqrt{3x+1}+\\sqrt{7})}{3(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{3x+1}+\\sqrt{7}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3(2)+1}+\\sqrt{7}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}+\\sqrt{7}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186237817 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}-x}{{x}^{2}-3} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(x-\\frac{1}{x})}{(1-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{x-0}{1-0} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }x \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186237818 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{6}+7{x}^{3}-9}{2{x}^{3}-1} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{6}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(2{x}^{3}+7-\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})}{(2-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{3}+7-0}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{3}+7) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186237819 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x-1}{3x+2} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{x}+\\frac{x}{x}-\\frac{1}{x})}{(\\frac{3x}{x}+\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(2x+1-\\frac{1}{x})}{(3+\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(2x+1-0)}{(3+0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(2x+1) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."] [17592186237820 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{4}+x-2}{6x-5} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{8{x}^{4}}{x}+\\frac{x}{x}-\\frac{2}{x})}{(\\frac{6x}{x}-\\frac{5}{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(8{x}^{3}+1-\\frac{2}{x})}{(6-\\frac{5}{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(8{x}^{3}+1-0)}{(6-0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(8{x}^{3}+1) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."] [17592186237855 :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{2(-2)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2{a}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2{a}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(z+3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{(z+3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{z+3}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{a+3}+\\frac{1}{0+3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|]_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡a-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nIn conclusion, only integrals $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent."] [17592186237856 :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{u-1}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(1+\\frac{1}{a-1}) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(0-{a}^{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{a}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x-2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x-2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[4\\sqrt{x-2}]}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =4\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(\\sqrt{a-2}-\\sqrt{3-2}) \\\\  & =4\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(\\sqrt{a-2}-1) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\nIn conclusion, only integrals $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}t\\,\\text{d}t\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x-2}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ are divergent."] [17592186237869 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the function $q(x)$ is differentiable for all $x\\ne 1.$ Therefore, the correct answer is $q(x)=\\begin{cases}x-1 & x<1 \\\\ x+1, & x\\ge 1\\end{cases}.$\n\nAll other functions are not differentiable at $x=0.$\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=0$, so it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ has a corner point at $x=0$, so it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $p(x)$ has a vertical tangent at  $x=0$, so it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is not defined at $x=0$, so it is not differentiable there."] [17592186237873 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}2x-2, & x<0 \\\\ -2, & x\\ge 0\\end{cases}\\,\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186237873 :question/worked-solution "- Statement “It is continuous at $x=0$” is true  Indeed, let's investigate the continuity of $f(x).$ It is continuous everywhere except, possibly, at  $x=0.$ At this point, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(2x-2)=2(0)-2=-2 \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}-2=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=f(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$ .\n\n- Statement “It is differentiable at $x=0$” is false, $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0$ because it has a corner point there: \n\n$\\,$ ${f}^{′}(x)=2\\,$  if  $\\,x<0$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0\\,$ if $\\,x>0$\n\n- Statement “It is differentiable on the interval $(0,2)$” is true. Indeed, $f(x)$ is differentiable everywhere except at $x=0.$   Since $x=0$  doesn't belong to $(0,2)$, the given function is differentiable on this interval.\n\nTherefore, only statements “It is continuous at $x=0$” and “It is differentiable on the interval $(0,2)$” are true."] [17592186240786 :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$” is true. We have $f(2)=2.$\n\n- Statement $\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)\\ne f(2)$ is false. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=2=f(2).$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2$” is false. The graph of the function doesn't have a break at $x=2$ and we can draw the graph of $f(x)$ around $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper."] [17592186240787 :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=5$” is true. Indeed, $f(5)=2.$\n\n- Statement “$\\lim_{x\\to \\,5}f(x)$ exists” is false. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{5}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{5}^{+}}f(x) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,  $\\lim_{x\\to \\,5}f(x)$ doesn't exist\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=5$” is true. The graph of the function has a jump exactly at $x=5$\nand it's impossible to draw the graph without taking our pencil off of the paper."] [17592186240788 :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is **not** defined at $x=0$” is true. From the graph we can see that no value has been assigned to $f(x)$ at $x=0.$\n\n- Statement “$\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)$ exists” is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x) & =3, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x) & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n$\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$” is false. The function is not even defined at $x=0.$ The graph contains a break at $x=0.$ It's impossible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper."]}
